<?xml version="1.0"?>
<Definitions xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
    <Fonts>
        <!--
            The symbols a screen needs that letters cannot draw, and that Space Engineers does not have.
            It has no undo, no redo and no delete glyph; its only cross is button_close_symbol, which
            means dismiss rather than destroy.

            A font rather than a set of sprites, because a screen and a text panel both draw text and
            both resolve a font by subtype out of the same definition registry - where a sprite wants a
            material declared once per target per icon, in files that then have to be kept agreeing.

            No ColorMask, so a symbol takes whatever colour it is drawn in - the same arrangement the
            game's own White uses, and the reason its Blue exists as a separate definition at all.

            THIS BELONGS IN A MOD OF ITS OWN. It is meant to be depended on by any display mod rather
            than copied into each, and nothing about it is named for the mod it currently travels in. It
            lives here while the glyph set settles and must be lifted out before this mod is published,
            or the second mod to want a bin icon will ship a second one that disagrees.
        -->
        <Font>
            <Id>
                <TypeId>FontDefinition</TypeId>
                <SubtypeId>MaltekSymbols</SubtypeId>
            </Id>
            <Path>Fonts\Maltek\MaltekSymbols.xml</Path>
        </Font>
        <!--
            A fixed-width face, for anything read as a listing rather than as prose. A saved
            program above all: a column of line numbers and a column of indentation line up only
            if every character is the same width. The game ships a monospace face of its own and
            it is not pleasant to read at this size.

            Cut to the same line height and baseline as the game's own faces, so a line of it
            occupies exactly the height of a line anywhere else and sits on the same baseline.

            The letterforms are Roboto Mono, by the Roboto Mono Project Authors, under the SIL
            Open Font License 1.1 - see Fonts\Maltek\OFL.txt, which ships because the licence
            requires every copy to carry the notice and the licence. It is not called Roboto Mono
            because a subtype here is a lookup key rather than a byline, and the rasterising is
            ours to answer for.

            THIS BELONGS IN THE SAME MOD THE SYMBOLS DO, for the same reason.
        -->
        <Font>
            <Id>
                <TypeId>FontDefinition</TypeId>
                <SubtypeId>MaltekMono</SubtypeId>
            </Id>
            <Path>Fonts\Maltek\MaltekMono.xml</Path>
        </Font>
    </Fonts>
</Definitions>
